{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bcb0b4cf98223397", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tarXiv:2608.08569v1 [cs.AI] 9 Aug 2026\n2\t\n3\tVoxZip: Semantic-Anchored Temporal KV Cache Compression for\n4\tLong-Context Audio Inference\n5\tWenxu Jia∗\n6\t\n7\tDongjie Fu∗\n8\t\n9\tXize Cheng∗\n10\t\n11\tjiawenxu@zju.edu.cn\n12\t1 Zhejiang University\n13\tHangzhou, China\n14\t2 Meituan\n15\tShanghai, China\n16\t\n17\tfudongjie@zju.edu.cn\n18\t1 Zhejiang University\n19\tHangzhou, China\n20\t\n21\tchengxize@zju.edu.cn\n22\t1 Zhejiang University\n23\tHangzhou, China\n24\t\n25\tFangming Feng∗\n26\t\n27\tLinjun Li\n28\t\n29\tWenshi Chen\n30\t\n31\tfangmingfeng@zju.edu.cn\n32\t1 Zhejiang University\n33\tHangzhou, China\n34\t\n35\tlilinjun05@meituan.com\n36\t2 Meituan\n37\tShanghai, China\n38\t\n39\twenshi.chen@meituan.com\n40\t2 Meituan\n41\tShanghai, China\n42\t\n43\tYingming Li\n44\t\n45\tZhou Zhao\n46\t\n47\tTao Jin†\n48\t\n49\tyingming@zju.edu.cn\n50\t1 Zhejiang University\n51\tHangzhou, China\n52\t\n53\tzhaozhou@zju.edu.cn\n54\t1 Zhejiang University\n55\tHangzhou, China\n56\t\n57\t1 Zhejiang University\n58\t\n59\tjint_zju@zju.edu.cn\n60\tHangzhou, China\n61\t\n62\tAbstract\n63\t\n64\tCCS Concepts\n65\t\n66\tRecent advancements in Speech Large Language Models have\n67\tdemonstrated remarkable capabilities in understanding complex\n68\taudio tasks. Despite this progress, their long-context inference\n69\tremains severely bottlenecked by prohibitive KV cache memory\n70\tdemands. Existing text-centric compression methods struggle here,\n71\toften disrupting speech continuity or discarding crucial semantic\n72\tcues. To address this, we propose VoxZip, a train-free, two-stage\n73\tsemantic-anchored KV cache compression framework. The first\n74\tstage uses automatic speech recognition (ASR) transcriptions\n75\tas explicit semantic anchors to temporally align, compress, and\n76\tfuse audio tokens, significantly reducing the initial KV cache\n77\twhile elevating token information density. To further improve the\n78\tcompression ratio, the second stage employs a dynamic filtering\n79\tstrategy based on temporally decayed accumulated attention\n80\tto evict non-essential tokens while mitigating early-token bias.\n81\tComprehensive evaluations on Qwen3-Omni across six diverse\n82\taudio benchmarks demonstrate the superiority of our approach.\n83\tVoxZip excels in long-audio reasoning and consistently maintains\n84\thigh-fidelity perception on short-form tasks. Notably, it sustains\n85\tover 90% of the uncompressed baseline performance even under an\n86\taggressive 20x KV cache compression in long-context scenarios.\n87\tFurthermore, at a 4x compression ratio, VoxZip yields a 1.9x\n88\tincrease in inference throughput alongside a 3.3x reduction in\n89\tpeak memory overhead. Code and models will be available at\n90\thttps://github.com/MM-Speech/VoxZip.\n91\t\n92\t• Computing methodologies → Artificial intelligence; Natural language processing.\n93\t\n94\t∗ The first four authors contributed equally to this work.\n95\t† Corresponding author: Tao Jin (jint_zju@zju.edu.cn).\n96\t\n97\tThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.\n98\tMM ’26, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n99\t© 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\n100\tACM ISBN 979-8-4007-2213-4/2026/11\n101\thttps://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835719\n102\t\n103\tKeywords\n104\tKV Cache Compression, Speech Large Language Models, Semantic\n105\tAnchor, Efficient Inference\n106\tACM Reference Format:\n107\tWenxu Jia, Dongjie Fu, Xize Cheng, Fangming Feng, Linjun Li, Wenshi Chen,\n108\tYingming Li, Zhou Zhao, and Tao Jin. 2026. VoxZip: Semantic-Anchored\n109\tTemporal KV Cache Compression for Long-Context Audio Inference. In\n110\tProceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM\n111\t’26), November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. ACM, New York, NY, USA,\n112\t10 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835719\n113\t\n114\t1\n115\t\n116\tIntroduction\n117\t\n118\tSpeech Large Language Models (SLLMs) have advanced rapidly\n119\t[12, 21]. The shift from cascade pipelines to unified end-to-end architectures strengthens the perception of multi-dimensional audio\n120\tinformation, including linguistic content, acoustics, and paralinguistic cues [9, 31, 32], enabling applications such as intelligent\n121\tcustomer service [7] and long-form meeting abstraction. However,\n122\tlong-context SLLM inference remains severely bottlenecked by\n123\tcomputational and memory costs [22]. Beyond model size and attention complexity, the KV cache accumulated during prefilling\n124\tand generation incurs prohibitive memory overhead [14, 16, 34],\n125\tmaking aggressive KV cache compression without compromising\n126\tholistic understanding a critical open challenge.\n127\tKV cache compression has shown strong potential in text and\n128\tvision, with mainstream eviction strategies relying on accumulated\n129\tattention scores or fixed windows for token selection [4, 15, 25–\n130\t27, 29]. Yet directly applying them to Speech LLMs is difficult due\n131\tto the temporal redundancy and low information density of acoustic signals. As shown in Figure 1, attention distributions exhibit\n132\t\n133\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n134\t\n135\tWenxu Jia et al.\n136\t\n137\tpronounced modal heterogeneity, with textual regions yielding\n138\thighly salient attention matrices, whereas raw audio tokens suffer\n139\tsevere attention dilution and distribution diffusion. This lack of\n140\tdistinctiveness makes attention-score-only KV compression unreliable for pinpointing critical semantic anchors in long-form audio,\n141\tfrequently causing semantic drift or hallucinations.\n142\t\n143\thigh performance stability even under extreme compression\n144\tconstraints.\n145\t• Extensive evaluations across six benchmarks show VoxZip\n146\tconsistently outperforms baselines, retaining over 90% performance at a 20× compression ratio, with a 1.93× throughput boost and a 3.34× reduction in peak memory usage.\n147\t\n148\t2 Related Works\n149\t2.1 Speech Large Language Models\n150\t\n151\tFigure 1: Attention score distributions in Qwen3-OmniInstruct-30B. Left: the uncompressed baseline shows highly\n152\tsparse attention over raw audio and text tokens. Right:\n153\tsemantic-anchored compression densifies and focuses the\n154\tattention.\n155\tTo address these challenges, we propose VoxZip, a train-free,\n156\ttwo-stage compression framework that reshapes information density via semantic anchoring. In the first stage, VoxZip treats ASRtranscribed text as explicit semantic anchors and leverages their\n157\ttimestamps to temporally align and extract the corresponding\n158\tacoustic intervals; fusing these raw audio representations with\n159\tanchor embeddings condenses redundant speech into high-density\n160\tsemantic segments. In the second stage, a time-decayed accumulated attention score metric dynamically prunes cached tokens,\n161\tmitigating the accumulation bias of early tokens to selectively safeguard essential semantic and acoustic cues. Consequently, VoxZip\n162\tsustains robust linguistic comprehension and paralinguistic perception under a minimal memory footprint.\n163\tWe evaluate VoxZip extensively across long-audio reasoning\n164\tbenchmarks (Vox-Infinity [5], AudioMarathon [11], SPIRAL [17])\n165\tand general audio-language benchmarks (MMSU [28], MMAU [24],\n166\tMMAR [20]). Results show that VoxZip achieves a superior trade-off\n167\tbetween perception fidelity and inference efficiency, maintaining robust performance under extreme context lengths while significantly\n168\talleviating the memory bottlenecks of SLLMs. Extended evaluations\n169\tfurther confirm robustness against acoustic disturbances, ensuring stable inference even when environmental noise compromises\n170\tthe ASR-guided anchors. The main contributions are summarized\n171\tbelow.\n172\t• A semantic-anchored audio compression mechanism that\n173\tcompresses semantic audio segments via textual anchors,\n174\tshrinking the initial KV cache to accelerate prefill while\n175\tboosting semantic reasoning in long-context scenarios.\n176\t• A KV cache eviction strategy based on temporally decayed accumulated attention scores, which mitigates the early-token\n177\taccumulation bias prevalent in long contexts and ensures\n178\t\n179\tRecent research in SLLMs has driven a paradigm shift from traditional cascaded pipelines to unified end-to-end architectures\n180\t[12, 21]. Foundational models such as Whisper have established robust semantic representations of speech through large-scale weakly\n181\tsupervised pre-training [23] . Subsequently, advanced frameworks\n182\tsuch as Qwen-Omni [30, 31], Audio Flamingo [9, 10], and KimiAudio [3, 8, 13] have further expanded the boundaries of speech\n183\treasoning. Beyond precise linguistic transcription, these models\n184\tdemonstrate an enhanced capability to perceive nuanced paralinguistic cues, such as emotion, speaker traits, and environmental\n185\tacoustics [6]. However, because end-to-end models must directly\n186\tprocess audio features that significantly longer token representations, critical system bottlenecks emerge as application scenarios\n187\tscale to long-context tasks like extensive meeting summarization\n188\tand ultra-multi-turn dialogues [1, 5, 11]. The quadratic computational complexity of the self-attention mechanism, coupled with the\n189\tlinear memory accumulation of the Key-Value (KV) cache, imposes\n190\tsevere memory overheads [22]. Consequently, these architectural\n191\tconstraints significantly hinder the efficient inference and realtime deployment of long-form audio tasks on resource-constrained\n192\thardware.\n193\t\n194\t2.2\n195\t\n196\tKV Cache Compression in LLMs\n197\t\n198\tTo mitigate the memory overhead of large language models (LLMs)\n199\tduring inference, the research community initially proposed various\n200\tKV cache compression strategies in the text modality [14, 19, 34].\n201\tEarly works such as StreamingLLM [29] and SnapKV [15] primarily\n202\tfocused on token-level attention sink mechanisms and importance\n203\tsampling. Subsequently, methods like PyramidKV exploited the\n204\theterogeneous attention patterns across network layers to achieve\n205\tlayer-wise compression [2], while ChunkKV [18] explored a coarsegrained compression paradigm based on semantic chunks . With\n206\tthe rapid evolution of multimodal models, compression techniques\n207\tin the vision domain have also emerged. For instance, FastV [4]\n208\tand DyCoke [25] designed dynamic pruning strategies tailored to\n209\tthe spatial-temporal redundancy of images and videos, establishing\n210\trobust memory optimization paradigms for efficient long-context\n211\tvisual reasoning.\n212\tHowever, directly transferring compression techniques from\n213\ttext or vision modalities to the audio domain is challenging due\n214\tto the inherent temporal redundancy and low information density\n215\tof acoustic signals. Currently, KV cache compression for Speech\n216\tLLMs remains largely underexplored. While a recent study [17]\n217\tinvestigates token pruning for speech representations, it lacks a\n218\tdynamic eviction mechanism during the autoregressive decoding\n219\tstage, and its closed-source nature currently precludes direct empirical comparison. Our proposed VoxZip fills this critical gap. By\n220\t\n221\t\fVoxZip: Semantic-Anchored Audio Compression\n222\t\n223\tMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n224\t\n225\tleveraging ASR-guided semantic anchoring and introducing a dynamic filtering strategy with a temporal-decay mechanism to evict\n226\tlow information tokens during decoding, we achieve substantial\n227\tcompression ratios across multiple long-audio benchmarks while\n228\teffectively preserving the model’s inherent comprehension and\n229\tperception capabilities.\n230\t\n231\t3 Methodology\n232\t3.1 Preliminaries: KV Cache in Speech LLMs\n233\tIn standard SLLMs, the generative inference process typically consists of two main stages, namely the prompt prefilling stage and\n234\tthe auto-regressive decoding stage. To avoid the redundant computation of historical states, SLLMs store past Key and Value representations in GPU memory, forming the KV cache.\n235\tPrefilling stage. Given raw audio and textual inputs, the model\n236\tfirst extracts the corresponding acoustic and textual embeddings.\n237\tThese embeddings are then processed by the Transformer layers of\n238\tthe SLLM to compute the initial key and value matrices, denoted\n239\tas 𝐾0 and 𝑉0 . These matrices are stored to initialize the KV cache\n240\tfor the entire prompt sequence.\n241\tDecoding stage. The model generates new tokens autoregressively. At time step 𝑡, for the newly generated token 𝑥𝑡 ,\n242\tthe model only computes its corresponding query 𝑞𝑡 , key 𝑘𝑡 , and\n243\tvalue 𝑣𝑡 ∈ R1×𝑑 , where 𝑑 is the hidden dimension. The model\n244\tthen retrieves the historical KV cache 𝐾𝑡 −1 and 𝑉𝑡 −1 , dynamically\n245\tupdating it by concatenating the new representations:\n246\t𝐾𝑡 = [𝐾𝑡 −1, 𝑘𝑡 ],\n247\t\n248\t𝑉𝑡 = [𝑉𝑡 −1, 𝑣𝑡 ]\n249\t\n250\t(1)\n251\t\n252\there [·, ·] denotes the concatenation operation along the sequence dimension. The attention output 𝑜𝑡 for the current step\n253\tis subsequently computed using the updated cache:\n254\t\u0013\n255\t𝑞𝑡 𝐾𝑡⊤\n256\t𝑜𝑡 = Softmax √\n257\t𝑉𝑡\n258\t(2)\n259\t𝑑\n260\tConsequently, the size of the KV cache grows linearly with the\n261\tlength of the input and generated sequences. This O (𝐿) linear expansion significantly increases inference latency and memory footprint, particularly in long-context scenarios. Furthermore, unlike\n262\thighly discrete text sequences, raw audio inputs exhibit extreme\n263\ttemporal redundancy, often producing massive audio tokens even\n264\tfor short utterances. Therefore, developing an efficient, modalityaware KV cache compression strategy is imperative to mitigate the\n265\tmemory bottleneck in SLLMs.\n266\t\n267\tUnlike discrete text sequences, continuous audio streams exhibit\n268\tlower information density and typically lack highly distinguishable semantic boundaries. This characteristic severely limits the\n269\tperformance of SLLMs in long-audio reasoning and retrieval tasks.\n270\tTo address this issue and enhance the model’s comprehension of\n271\tlong contexts, we propose a semantic-anchored audio compression\n272\tmethod. This approach utilizes transcribed text as explicit guides\n273\tto compress the corresponding acoustic intervals, thereby significantly elevating the information density of these semantic regions.\n274\tSpecifically, the first-stage compression comprises three steps.\n275\tTemporal-Aligned Embedding Partitioning. Given a raw\n276\taudio waveform input 𝐴𝑟𝑎𝑤 with a total duration of 𝜏𝑡𝑜𝑡𝑎𝑙 , we\n277\tutilize an auxiliary ASR model to generate initial transcriptions.\n278\tTo ensure robustness against environmental noise, we only retain transcribed segments with a confidence score above a threshold of 0.3. The remaining high-confidence transcriptions constitute the valid semantic set S = {𝑆 1, . . . , 𝑆𝑛 }, where each segment\n279\t(𝑖 )\n280\t(𝑖 )\n281\t𝑆𝑖 = (𝜏𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡\n282\t, 𝜏𝑒𝑛𝑑\n283\t, text (𝑖 ) ) contains the start time, end time, and the\n284\tcorresponding text. This preemptive filtering prevents erroneous semantic anchors from corrupting the continuous audio latent space.\n285\tConcurrently, the SLLM encodes the raw audio 𝐴𝑟𝑎𝑤 into a continuous acoustic feature sequence 𝐸𝐴 ∈ R𝐿𝑎 ×𝑑 , while embedding\n286\t(𝑖 )\n287\t\n288\teach valid transcribed text text (𝑖 ) into 𝐸𝑇(𝑖 ) ∈ R𝐿𝑡 ×𝑑 . Here, 𝐿𝑎 and\n289\t𝐿𝑡(𝑖 ) denote their respective sequence lengths, and 𝑑 represents the\n290\tshared hidden dimension.\n291\tCrucially, instead of physically truncating the raw audio waveform, we slice directly within the extracted continuous acoustic\n292\tlatent space 𝐸𝐴 . Given the strong continuity of speech, this featurelevel segmentation preserves the complete global acoustic context.\n293\tBased on the obtained timestamps, we map the absolute time to\n294\tdiscrete audio token indices:\n295\t\n296\t\u0012\n297\t\n298\t3.2\n299\t\n300\tThe Proposed VoxZip\n301\t\n302\tIn this section, we present VoxZip. As illustrated in Figure 2, VoxZip\n303\temploys a two-stage compression strategy. The first stage compresses audio tokens under the guidance of semantic anchors. The\n304\tsecond stage further dynamically filters out low-information tokens\n305\tbased on temporally decayed accumulated attention scores. This\n306\tapproach aims to preserve model performance while reducing the\n307\ttoken count as extensively as possible.\n308\t3.2.1 Semantic-Anchored Audio Compression. As shown in Figure\n309\t1, previous observations indicate that the attention of audio input in SLLMs suffers from attention dilution over long sequences.\n310\t\n311\t(𝑖 )\n312\t𝑖𝑑𝑥𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡\n313\t= ⌊𝐿𝑎 ·\n314\t\n315\t!\n316\t(𝑖 )\n317\t𝜏𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡\n318\t⌋,\n319\t𝜏𝑡𝑜𝑡𝑎𝑙\n320\t\n321\t(𝑖 )\n322\t𝑖𝑑𝑥𝑒𝑛𝑑\n323\t= ⌊𝐿𝑎 ·\n324\t\n325\t(𝑖 )\n326\t𝜏𝑒𝑛𝑑\n327\t\n328\t!\n329\t⌋\n330\t\n331\t(3)\n332\t\n333\t𝜏𝑡𝑜𝑡𝑎𝑙\n334\t\n335\tUsing these indices, we slice 𝐸𝐴 to extract the semantic audio\n336\t(𝑖 )\n337\t(𝑖 )\n338\t(𝑖 )\n339\tintervals, denoted as 𝐸𝐴,𝑠\n340\t= 𝐸𝐴 [𝑖𝑑𝑥𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡\n341\t: 𝑖𝑑𝑥𝑒𝑛𝑑\n342\t]. The remaining\n343\tparts of the sequence, which lack explicit semantic boundaries\n344\tand are not covered by any transcribed intervals, are identified as\n345\t(1)\n346\t(𝑚)\n347\tbackground acoustic intervals and denoted as {𝐸𝐴,𝑏𝑔\n348\t, . . . , 𝐸𝐴,𝑏𝑔\n349\t}.\n350\tSemantic-Anchored Audio Compression. For the 𝑖-th seman(𝑖 )\n351\t\n352\t(𝑖 )\n353\t(𝑖 )\n354\ttic audio interval, let 𝐸𝐴,𝑠\n355\t= [𝑒𝑎,1\n356\t, . . . , 𝑒 (𝑖 ) (𝑖 ) ] ∈ R𝐿𝑎 ×𝑑 denote its\n357\t𝑎,𝐿𝑎\n358\t(𝑖 )\n359\t(𝑖 )\n360\tacoustic feature sequence, and let 𝐸𝑇(𝑖 ) = [𝑒𝑡,1\n361\t, . . . , 𝑒 (𝑖 ) (𝑖 ) ] ∈ R𝐿𝑡 ×𝑑\n362\t𝑡,𝐿\n363\t𝑡\n364\t\n365\tdenote its corresponding text feature sequence. Since the audio sequence length 𝐿𝑎(𝑖 ) is typically much larger than the text sequence\n366\tlength 𝐿𝑡(𝑖 ) , we uniformly partition the 𝐿𝑎(𝑖 ) acoustic features into\n367\t𝐿𝑡(𝑖 ) consecutive groups along the temporal dimension. For the 𝑗-th\n368\tgroup G𝑖,𝑗 , we perform an element-wise average pooling to obtain\n369\t(𝑖 )\n370\tthe compressed acoustic representation 𝑒˜𝑎,𝑗\n371\t:\n372\t(𝑖 )\n373\t𝑒˜𝑎,𝑗\n374\t=\n375\t\n376\t∑︁\n377\t1\n378\t(𝑖 )\n379\t𝑒𝑎,𝑘\n380\t|G𝑖,𝑗 |\n381\t𝑘 ∈ G𝑖,𝑗\n382\t\n383\t(4)\n384\t\n385\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n386\t\n387\tWenxu Jia et al.\n388\t\n389\t[start time\n390\t,end time\n391\t]\n392\t: \"Hello, how are you?\"\n393\t\n394\t,end time\n395\t: \"goodbye~\"\n396\t\n397\tASR\n398\t\n399\tASR Text\n400\tEmbedding\n401\t\n402\t:\n403\t\n404\tBackgroud\n405\tSegment\n406\tNo ASR\n407\tInterleave\n408\t\n409\tSemantic\n410\tSegment\n411\tASR\n412\t\n413\t.\n414\t.\n415\t.\n416\t\n417\t:\n418\t\n419\tAttention sink\n420\t\n421\tRetain\n422\traw audio\n423\tembeddings\n424\t\n425\tASR\n426\tModel\n427\t\n428\t.\n429\t.\n430\t.\n431\t\n432\tLLM\n433\tPooling\n434\t\n435\tRecent\n436\ttoken\n437\t\n438\t0.9\n439\t0.1 0.9\n440\t\n441\tAttention\n442\tscore\n443\t\n444\t0.2 0.2 0.9\n445\t\n446\tCompressed Audio\n447\tEmbedding\n448\t\n449\t0.4 0.4 0.5 0.9\n450\t0.4 0.4 0.3 0.8 0.9\n451\t\n452\tPooling\n453\t\n454\tRetain\n455\tAudio Input\n456\t\n457\tCompressed Audio Embeding\n458\t\n459\tCached Tokens\n460\tSemantic\n461\tSegment\n462\t\n463\t]\n464\t\n465\tRaw Audio Embedding\n466\t\n467\tFused Embedding\n468\t\n469\tCompressed Embedding\n470\t\n471\tTimestamp\n472\t\n473\t.\n474\t.\n475\t.\n476\t[start time\n477\t\n478\tASR Text Embedding\n479\t\n480\tAsr time bar\n481\t\n482\tStage 1: Prefill stage Time aligned\n483\t\n484\t.\n485\t.\n486\t.\n487\t\n488\tAudio\n489\tEncoder\n490\t\n491\tRaw Audio Embedding\n492\t\n493\t1.51 1.92 1.58 1.67 0.9\n494\t\n495\tStage 2:\n496\tDecoding stage\n497\t\n498\tTop k filter\n499\t\n500\tThe compressed cache\n501\t\n502\tFigure 2: The VoxZip framework. Stage 1 (left) performs semantic-anchored audio compression during the prefill stage. Stage 2\n503\t(right) dynamically filters the KV cache using time-decayed attention scores during decoding.\n504\twhere |G𝑖,𝑗 | is the number of audio tokens in the group. Subsequently, we perform an element-wise addition between this com(𝑖 )\n505\tpressed acoustic representation 𝑒˜𝑎,𝑗\n506\tand its aligned text embedding\n507\t(𝑖 )\n508\t(𝑖 )\n509\t(𝑖 )\n510\t𝑒𝑡,𝑗\n511\tto construct the semantic anchor 𝑒 𝑓(𝑖,𝑗) = 𝑒˜𝑎,𝑗\n512\t+ 𝑒𝑡,𝑗\n513\t. This operation compresses the audio semantic interval into a high-density\n514\t(𝑖 )\n515\t\n516\tsequence 𝐸 𝑓(𝑖 ) = [𝑒 𝑓(𝑖,1) , . . . , 𝑒 (𝑖 ) (𝑖 ) ] ∈ R𝐿𝑡 ×𝑑 , naturally preserving\n517\t𝑓 ,𝐿𝑡\n518\t\n519\tcrucial paralinguistic cues while tightly aligning with core textual\n520\tsemantics.\n521\tBackground Preservation and Sequence Concatenation.\n522\t(1)\n523\t(𝑚)\n524\tFor the background acoustic intervals {𝐸𝐴,𝑏𝑔\n525\t, . . . , 𝐸𝐴,𝑏𝑔\n526\t}, we preserve their original acoustic features to maintain the model’s perception of global environmental sounds. Finally, we concatenate all\n527\tthe fused semantic embedding intervals 𝐸 𝑓(𝑖 ) and the preserved back(𝑘 )\n528\tground embedding intervals 𝐸𝐴,𝑏𝑔\n529\tin their original chronological\n530\torder to form the final compressed input sequence 𝐸𝑐𝑜𝑚𝑝 :\n531\t(𝑘 )\n532\t𝐸𝑐𝑜𝑚𝑝 = Concat(. . . , 𝐸 𝑓(𝑖 ) , 𝐸𝐴,𝑏𝑔\n533\t, 𝐸 𝑓(𝑖+1) , . . . )\n534\t\n535\t(5)\n536\t\n537\tThrough this strategy, the length of the transcribed audio intervals is compressed to approximately 0.25× of their original size.\n538\tThis significant reduction accelerates the prefill stage and substantially reduces the memory footprint of the initial KV cache. Furthermore, the introduction of semantic anchors effectively enhances the\n539\tmodel’s semantic reasoning and retrieval capabilities in long-audio\n540\tcontexts.\n541\t3.2.2 Temporally Decayed KV Cache Eviction. Following the first\n542\tstage, the KV cache comprises both high-density semantic tokens\n543\tand raw acoustic tokens. To further prune redundant and low information elements, we propose a dynamic KV cache eviction mechanism during the generation stage. Mainstream score-based eviction\n544\tmethods typically evaluate token importance via simple accumulation of historical attention scores [2, 15, 33]. However, directly\n545\t\n546\tapplying this to long-form audio induces a severe accumulation\n547\tbias. Since early tokens exist for a longer duration and undergo\n548\tmore accumulation steps, they inherently amass higher scores, causing the eviction mechanism to persistently select and retain them.\n549\tTo mitigate this bias and preserve paralinguistic sensitivity, we\n550\tintroduce a temporal decay mechanism.\n551\tSpecifically, operating independently per layer, the time-decayed\n552\taccumulated score vector 𝐴 (𝑡 ) ∈ R1×𝑡 is dynamically updated using\n553\tthe 𝑡-th query 𝑞𝑡 ∈ R1×𝑑 and the current key cache 𝐾𝑡 ∈ R𝑡 ×𝑑 :\n554\t\u0013\n555\t\u0012\n556\t𝑞𝑡 𝐾 ⊤\n557\t(6)\n558\t𝐴 (𝑡 ) = 𝛾 · [𝐴 (𝑡 −1) , 0] + Softmax √ 𝑡\n559\t𝑑\n560\twhere 𝑑 represents the head dimension, 𝛾 ∈ (0, 1) is a predefined\n561\tdecay coefficient, and [·, ·] denotes zero-padding concatenation to\n562\talign the dimension for the newly generated token. This formulation evaluates the global historical contribution of tokens while\n563\texplicitly penalizing stale acoustic states.\n564\tDuring eviction, the cache is partitioned into an attention sink\n565\twindow, a recent window, and important historical tokens. To maintain generation stability, we unconditionally preserve the first 𝑇\n566\tattention sink tokens and the latest 𝑀 recent tokens. For the intermediate historical tokens, we extract the top-𝑁 elements based\n567\ton their decayed accumulated score 𝐴 (𝑡 ) . In our implementation,\n568\twe empirically set 𝑇 = 4 and dynamically allocate 20% of the total\n569\tKV cache budget to the recent window 𝑀, assigning the remaining\n570\tcapacity to 𝑁 . Finally, the updated KV cache is constructed via\n571\tconcatenation:\n572\t𝐾𝑡 +1 = Concat(𝐾𝑡 [: 𝑇 , :], 𝐾𝑡 [Iℎ𝑖𝑠𝑡 , :], 𝐾𝑡 [−𝑀 :, :])\n573\t\n574\t(7)\n575\t\n576\t𝑉𝑡 +1 = Concat(𝑉𝑡 [: 𝑇 , :], 𝑉𝑡 [Iℎ𝑖𝑠𝑡 , :], 𝑉𝑡 [−𝑀 :, :])\n577\t\n578\t(8)\n579\t\n580\t\u0011\n581\t\u0010\n582\tIℎ𝑖𝑠𝑡 = Top𝑁 𝐴 (𝑡 ) [𝑇 : −𝑀]\n583\t\n584\t(9)\n585\t\n586\t\fVoxZip: Semantic-Anchored Audio Compression\n587\t\n588\tMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n589\t\n590\tTable 1: Performance on long-context audio benchmarks under varying KV cache budgets (%). Backbone: Qwen3-Omni-Instruct30B. Subsets: Beyond-Semantics Dialogue (BS), Conversational Dialogues (Conv), Ultra Multi-Turn Dialogue (UMT), Personal\n591\tMonologue (PM), AudioMarathon (SCE: Speech Content Extraction; AC: Audio Classification; SR: Speaker Recognition), and\n592\tSPIRAL Hard (H). Aver.: average accuracy. PRR: performance retention ratio vs. the Full KV baseline (%). Bold: best within each\n593\tbudget.\n594\t\n595\tMethod\n596\t\n597\tVox-Infinity\n598\t\n599\tRatio\n600\t\n601\tAudioMarathon\n602\t\n603\tSPIRAL\n604\t\n605\tBS\n606\t\n607\tConv\n608\t\n609\tUMT\n610\t\n611\tPM\n612\t\n613\tSCE\n614\t\n615\tAC\n616\t\n617\tSR\n618\t\n619\t(H)\n620\t\n621\tAver.\n622\t\n623\tPRR\n624\t\n625\tFull KV\n626\t\n627\t100%\n628\t\n629\t31.60\n630\t\n631\t95.60\n632\t\n633\t69.20\n634\t\n635\t71.40\n636\t\n637\t49.03\n638\t\n639\t54.82\n640\t\n641\t66.90\n642\t\n643\t98.00\n644\t\n645\t67.07\n646\t\n647\t100.00\n648\t\n649\tStreamingLLM\n650\tSnapKV\n651\tPyramidKV\n652\tChunkKV\n653\tVoxZip (Ours)\n654\t\n655\t25%\n656\t25%\n657\t25%\n658\t25%\n659\t25%\n660\t\n661\t28.40\n662\t24.30\n663\t24.80\n664\t23.10\n665\t26.60\n666\t\n667\t70.00\n668\t78.40\n669\t85.50\n670\t77.20\n671\t96.20\n6