it achieves a high compression ratio without sacrificing model performance.\n833\t\n834\t4 Experiments\n835\t4.1 Experimental Setup and Implementation\n836\tDetails\n837\t4.1.1 Benchmark. To evaluate VoxZip, we select two categories\n838\tof benchmarks. The first category, Long-Context Reasoning and\n839\tRetrieval, is headlined by Vox-Infinity [5], the most demanding\n840\tbenchmark requiring complex multi-turn semantic retrieval and\n841\treasoning across ultra-long audio, with its Personal-Monologue\n842\tsubset averaging over 20 minutes. This category also includes AudioMarathon [11] and the SPIRAL hard subset [17], which focus\n843\ton relatively straightforward single-turn long-audio understanding. The second category, General Audio Understanding, comprises\n844\tMMSU [28], MMAU [24], and MMAR [20], primarily utilized to\n845\tassess the model’s perception of fine-grained acoustic features and\n846\tparalinguistic cues in shorter contexts.\n847\t4.1.2 Baselines. We evaluated our approach against five baselines.\n848\tFull KV serves as the uncompressed baseline, which retains the\n849\tcomplete KV cache. For compressed baselines, we include SLLM\n850\t[29], which maintains performance by preserving attention sink\n851\ttokens and the most recent tokens. We further evaluate SnapKV\n852\t[15], a widely adopted method that selects the top-𝑘 tokens based\n853\ton accumulated attention scores. To capture more sophisticated\n854\tcompression paradigms, we introduced PyramidKV [2], which allocates KV cache budgets hierarchically across different layers,\n855\t\n856\tand ChunkKV [18], which retains the cache based on semantic\n857\tsegments.\n858\t4.1.3 Implementation Details. All experiments are conducted on a\n859\tsingle computing node equipped with 8 NVIDIA A100 (80GB) GPUs.\n860\tFor the ASR model configuration, we utilize Whisper-Turbo as the\n861\tASR model and Qwen3-Omni-30B (both Instruct and Thinking\n862\tvariants) as the backbone LLM. For the hyperparameter settings,\n863\tthe temporal decay factor 𝛾 used for attention score aggregation in\n864\tStage 2 is empirically set to 0.95.\n865\t\n866\t4.2\n867\t\n868\tPerformance on Long-Context Audio\n869\tBenchmarks\n870\t\n871\tTo evaluate the long-context retrieval and reasoning capabilities\n872\tof various compression algorithms, we evaluate the Qwen3-OmniInstruct-30B and Qwen3-Omni-Thinking-30B on Vox-Infinity, AudioMarathon, and the SPIRAL (Hard) subset. These benchmarks\n873\trigorously test the model’s ability to extract core semantic features\n874\tunder substantial KV cache constraints.\n875\tAs shown in Table 1, at a 25% cache budget, VoxZip consistently\n876\toutperforms all baselines and notably surpasses the uncompressed\n877\tFull KV baseline in average accuracy. This gain is fundamentally\n878\tdriven by introducing ASR transcriptions as explicit semantic anchors. Fusing these condensed textual features with aligned audio\n879\ttokens reconstructs the sequence’s information density. These injected textual priors provide reliable guidance, significantly enhancing long-audio semantic understanding while preserving essential\n880\tparalinguistic cues.\n881\tVoxZip maintains robust performance even under extreme compression. At a 10% cache budget, both the Instruct and Thinking\n882\tvariants retain over 90% of their uncompressed baseline, demonstrating the method’s robust generalization across distinct tuning\n883\t\n884\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n885\t\n886\tWenxu Jia et al.\n887\t\n888\tTable 2: Performance on general audio benchmarks (Qwen3-Omni-Instruct-30B). Ratio: KV cache budget (%). MMAR: Semantic,\n889\tCultural, Perception, Signal; MMSU: Semantics, Phonology, Style, Traits; MMAU: Sound, Speech, Music. Aver.: average accuracy.\n890\tPRR: performance retention ratio vs. Full KV (%).\n891\t\n892\tMethod\n893\t\n894\tMMAR\n895\t\n896\tRatio\n897\t\n898\tMMSU\n899\t\n900\tMMAU\n901\t\n902\tSem.\n903\t\n904\tCul.\n905\t\n906\tPer.\n907\t\n908\tSig.\n909\t\n910\tSem.\n911\t\n912\tPho.\n913\t\n914\tSty.\n915\t\n916\tTra.\n917\t\n918\tSnd.\n919\t\n920\tSpe.\n921\t\n922\tMus.\n923\t\n924\tAver.\n925\t\n926\tPRR\n927\t\n928\tFull KV\n929\t\n930\t100%\n931\t\n932\t70.56\n933\t\n934\t58.39\n935\t\n936\t55.45\n937\t\n938\t46.51\n939\t\n940\t81.64\n941\t\n942\t69.97\n943\t\n944\t54.03\n945\t\n946\t35.35\n947\t\n948\t80.48\n949\t\n950\t78.08\n951\t\n952\t74.25\n953\t\n954\t65.18\n955\t\n956\t100.0\n957\t\n958\tStreamingLLM\n959\tSnapKV\n960\tPyramidKV\n961\tChunkKV\n962\tVoxZip (Ours)\n963\t\n964\t25%\n965\t25%\n966\t25%\n967\t25%\n968\t25%\n969\t\n970\t47.69\n971\t39.17\n972\t55.47\n973\t44.53\n974\t72.51\n975\t\n976\t40.15\n977\t46.72\n978\t60.58\n979\t50.36\n980\t72.99\n981\t\n982\t38.12\n983\t37.13\n984\t48.51\n985\t45.30\n986\t67.24\n987\t\n988\t34.88\n989\t48.84\n990\t55.81\n991\t69.77\n992\t61.54\n993\t\n994\t57.86\n995\t38.45\n996\t51.79\n997\t38.65\n998\t72.35\n999\t\n1000\t54.44\n1001\t53.61\n1002\t72.92\n1003\t60.90\n1004\t63.72\n1005\t\n1006\t51.48\n1007\t40.43\n1008\t54.49\n1009\t61.11\n1010\t56.80\n1011\t\n1012\t35.48\n1013\t35.48\n1014\t42.61\n1015\t44.27\n1016\t37.53\n1017\t\n1018\t40.54\n1019\t25.23\n1020\t71.17\n1021\t74.47\n1022\t80.78\n1023\t\n1024\t36.23\n1025\t23.63\n1026\t65.87\n1027\t64.07\n1028\t75.48\n1029\t\n1030\t29.43\n1031\t22.52\n1032\t58.86\n1033\t50.45\n1034\t75.45\n1035\t\n1036\t42.39\n1037\t37.35\n1038\t58.01\n1039\t54.91\n1040\t66.93\n1041\t\n1042\t65.04\n1043\t57.30\n1044\t89.01\n1045\t84.24\n1046\t102.64\n1047\t\n1048\tVoxZip (Ours)\n1049\tVoxZip (Ours)\n1050\t\n1051\t15%\n1052\t10%\n1053\t\n1054\t72.75\n1055\t72.51\n1056\t\n1057\t70.80\n1058\t69.34\n1059\t\n1060\t66.09\n1061\t65.84\n1062\t\n1063\t58.14\n1064\t58.14\n1065\t\n1066\t72.49\n1067\t70.06\n1068\t\n1069\t65.06\n1070\t62.04\n1071\t\n1072\t56.74\n1073\t56.74\n1074\t\n1075\t37.53\n1076\t37.66\n1077\t\n1078\t80.48\n1079\t79.88\n1080\t\n1081\t75.45\n1082\t74.31\n1083\t\n1084\t75.77\n1085\t75.77\n1086\t\n1087\t66.48\n1088\t65.66\n1089\t\n1090\t102.01\n1091\t100.86\n1092\t\n1093\tarchitectures. Even at an extreme 5% KV cache budget, VoxZip sustains a 91.41% performance retention, significantly outperforming\n1094\tthe strongest baseline PyramidKV [2], which retains only 66.70% at a\n1095\tmuch looser 25% budget. This resilience stems from the framework’s\n1096\tsynergistic design, guided by the first-stage textual semantic anchors, with the second stage employing a temporally decayed accumulated attention mechanism to dynamically evict low-information\n1097\ttokens. This formulation effectively mitigates early-token bias, enabling aggressive pruning without compromising critical semantic\n1098\tand paralinguistic cues.\n1099\t\n1100\t4.3\n1101\t\n1102\tPerformance on General Audio QA\n1103\tBenchmarks\n1104\t\n1105\tBeyond long-range retrieval, we evaluate the acoustic perception\n1106\tprecision of Qwen3-Omni-Instruct-30B on general audio questionanswering (QA) benchmarks including MMAR [20], MMSU [28] and\n1107\tMMAU [24]. Unlike long-audio tasks, these focus on fine-grained\n1108\tfeatures within a 30-second window, such as speaker traits, emotional and prosodic nuances, and low-level signal characteristics.\n1109\tThis critically assesses whether VoxZip preserves the rich nonlinguistic cues inherently absent from discrete text transcriptions.\n1110\tAs illustrated in Table 2, under a 25% KV cache budget ratio,\n1111\tVoxZip achieves an average accuracy of 66.93%, surpassing the\n1112\tFull KV baseline by 1.75%. By contrast, competing KV compression\n1113\tschemes suffer notable degradation, with even the strongest PyramidKV baseline reaching only 58.01% average accuracy. Crucially,\n1114\tVoxZip maintains its superiority under extreme compression by sustaining average accuracies of 66.48% and 65.66% at stricter 15% and\n1115\t10% budgets respectively, thereby consistently outperforming the\n1116\tuncompressed Full KV baseline. Furthermore, VoxZip demonstrates\n1117\textraordinary proficiency in tasks reliant on complex acoustic profiling. Specifically, on the Perception and Signal subsets of MMAR,\n1118\tit achieves accuracy gains of over 10% against Full KV.\n1119\tWe attribute these performance gains over the uncompressed\n1120\tbaseline to our framework’s synergistic design. The first-stage\n1121\tfusion robustly preserves essential paralinguistic cues, while the\n1122\tsecond-stage dynamic filtering evicts redundant tokens to suppress\n1123\tirrelevant acoustic noise. This denoised representation enables the\n1124\t\n1125\tmodel to focus precisely on salient acoustic events, yielding superior accuracy under a constrained token budget.\n1126\t\n1127\t4.4\n1128\t\n1129\tAblation Studies\n1130\t\n1131\t4.4.1 Necessity of Semantic Anchors in Long Audio Understanding. To validate the necessity of semantic anchors in long audio\n1132\tunderstanding, we conducted an ablation study on three semanticfocused subsets of the Vox-Infinity benchmark. This dataset was\n1133\tspecifically selected as its ultra-long context rigorously tests the\n1134\tmodel’s ability to accurately retrieve critical semantics amidst massive acoustic noise.\n1135\tAs shown in Table 3, removing the semantic-anchored compression (w/o Semantic-Anchored Comp.) at a 15% budget leads to\n1136\tsevere performance degradation, with accuracy dropping to 6.4%\n1137\ton the longest PM subset. In contrast, integrating the semantic\n1138\tanchors (w/ Semantic-Anchored Comp.) not only prevents this collapse by boosting the PM performance to an exceptional 81.6%, but\n1139\talso elevates the overall average to 82.27%, directly surpassing the\n1140\tuncompressed Full KV baseline of 78.7%.\n1141\tThis capability to exceed the uncompressed upper bound fundamentally stems from the reconstruction of information density\n1142\tfacilitated by semantic anchors. As illustrated by the attention\n1143\tmaps in Figure 1, under unanchored pure audio conditions, the\n1144\tmodel’s attention toward low-density audio tokens is highly discrete and sparse. This sparsity leads to a severe loss of focus during\n1145\tlong-context reasoning. Conversely, introducing semantic anchors\n1146\tinjects highly condensed semantic priors into the continuous audio stream, densifying the originally scattered attention scores.\n1147\tThis mechanism effectively guides the model to bypass acoustic\n1148\tredundancy and concentrate strictly on anchor segments that encapsulate core semantics. Consequently, it fortifies the robustness of\n1149\tlong-range semantic retrieval and provides highly reliable scoring\n1150\tcriteria for the subsequent Stage-2 KV cache eviction.\n1151\t4.4.2 Necessity of Preserving Acoustic and Paralinguistic Cues. To\n1152\tvalidate the design rationale of our first-stage semantic anchored\n1153\tcompression, we conducted an ablation study across multiple benchmarks emphasizing acoustic and paralinguistic comprehension.\n1154\tSpecifically, this study evaluates its performance advantages over\n1155\t\n1156\t\fVoxZip: Semantic-Anchored Audio Compression\n1157\t\n1158\tMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1159\t\n1160\tTable 3: Ablation of semantic-anchored audio compression\n1161\ton Vox-Infinity semantic subsets. w/: with; w/o: without;\n1162\tComp.: compression. Accuracy (%).\n1163\tMethod\n1164\t\n1165\tConv\n1166\t\n1167\tUMT\n1168\t\n1169\tPM\n1170\t\n1171\tAvg.\n1172\t\n1173\tFull KV\n1174\t\n1175\t95.60\n1176\t\n1177\t69.20\n1178\t\n1179\t71.40\n1180\t\n1181\t78.70\n1182\t\n1183\tKV Cache Budget Ratio: 15%\n1184\tw/o Semantic-Anchored Comp.\n1185\tw/ Semantic-Anchored Comp.\n1186\t\n1187\t87.04\n1188\t98.00\n1189\t\n1190\t44.80\n1191\t67.20\n1192\t\n1193\t6.40\n1194\t81.60\n1195\t\n1196\t46.08\n1197\t82.27\n1198\t\n1199\tKV Cache Budget Ratio: 10%\n1200\tw/o Semantic-Anchored Comp.\n1201\tw/ Semantic-Anchored Comp.\n1202\t\n1203\t85.80\n1204\t98.10\n1205\t\n1206\t36.40\n1207\t55.00\n1208\t\n1209\t7.51\n1210\t76.50\n1211\t\n1212\t43.24\n1213\t76.53\n1214\tFigure 3: GPU peak memory across conversational turns.\n1215\t\n1216\tthe transcribed text-only baseline and the necessity of retaining\n1217\tun-transcribed pure audio segments during fusion. Table 4 presents\n1218\ta comprehensive performance comparison of the four evaluated\n1219\tmethods.\n1220\tFundamentally, the experimental results directly expose the inherent modality deficit of the Text-only baseline when processing\n1221\tcomplex audio tasks. While ASR models accurately extract semantic content, they inevitably strip away crucial paralinguistic cues\n1222\tsuch as emotion, speaker style, and environmental sounds. By contrast, our T+A (Full) strategy seamlessly integrates semantic and\n1223\tacoustic features, achieving an absolute average improvement of\n1224\t7.80% over the text-only baseline. Notably, in tasks heavily reliant\n1225\ton low-level acoustic perception, such as the Sign subset in MMAR\n1226\tand the Snd subset in MMAU, our method delivers absolute gains\n1227\tof 15.03% and 15.31% over the pure text baseline, respectively. This\n1228\tstrongly demonstrates that utilizing ASR transcriptions as anchors\n1229\tto compress and fuse audio features successfully preserves rich paralinguistic information while maintaining high semantic fidelity.\n1230\tFurthermore, the ablation validates the necessity of retaining\n1231\tthe un-transcribed audio segments. Under the T+A (Partial) configuration, which aggressively discards segments with no ASR output or low transcription confidence, the average accuracy drops\n1232\tfrom 60.76% to 57.41%. This degradation indicates that these nonsemantic audio intervals encode rich acoustic information and paralinguistic features essential for comprehensive scene and style\n1233\treasoning. By continuously preserving these untranscribed or lowconfidence segments, our T+A (Full) approach fully captures these\n1234\tunderlying acoustic cues, effectively preventing the loss of acoustic\n1235\tinformation that is typically ignored by ASR transcriptions.\n1236\tFinally, we reference the uncompressed Audio-only input as\n1237\tthe theoretical performance upper bound. Although the Audioonly baseline preserves the most comprehensive information and\n1238\tachieves the highest average accuracy of 62.84%, it concurrently\n1239\tincurs prohibitive KV cache overhead and inference latency. Encouragingly, our T+A (Full) approach maintains an exceptional\n1240\tperformance of 60.76% while compressing massive volumes of redundant speech frames down to the text-token length level. This\n1241\tstrongly suggests that our proposed semantic-anchored fusion strategy achieves a highly optimal balance between efficiency and accuracy, trading an exceptionally massive token compression rate for\n1242\ta negligible performance degradation.\n1243\t\n1244\t4.4.3 Necessity of the Temporal Decay Mechanism. To validate the\n1245\ttemporal decay (TD) mechanism, we ablate it on Qwen3-OmniInstruct-30B over Vox-Infinity under a highly constrained 5% KV\n1246\tcache budget, comparing TD against the standard accumulation\n1247\tbaseline (𝛾 = 1.0) and sweeping the decay factor 𝛾. As shown in\n1248\tTable 5, TD yields the largest gains on the Personal Monologues\n1249\t(PM) subset, whose long duration demands extended temporal reasoning. The non-TD baseline’s PM accuracy plummets to 62.6%,\n1250\twhile the optimal TD setting (𝛾 = 0.95) raises it to 76.5%, a 13.9%\n1251\tabsolute improvement.\n1252\tSweeping 𝛾 reveals an inverted-U trend: aggressive decay (𝛾 ≤\n1253\t0.90) prematurely discards historical semantic anchors, while excessively slow decay (𝛾 ≥ 0.99) under-penalizes stale audio tokens,\n1254\twith 𝛾 = 0.95 striking the optimal balance. By explicitly penalizing early tokens, TD mitigates the accumulation bias that plagues\n1255\tconventional score-based eviction, preserving a vital long-term\n1256\tperspective for token selection and reliably safeguarding reasoning\n1257\tstability under the extreme 5% budget.\n1258\t\n1259\t4.5\n1260\t\n1261\tInference Efficiency Analysis\n1262\t\n1263\tTo assess the practical computational efficiency of various compression strategies, we evaluate the Qwen3-Omni-30B-Thinking under\n1264\tlong-context multi-turn conversational scenarios. Table 6 details\n1265\tthe inference metrics under a fixed 64K context window, whereas\n1266\tFigure 3 tracks the dynamic peak memory accumulation across\n1267\t80 continuous conversational turns. Crucially, our reported peak\n1268\tmemory (Mem) and throughput (TPS) are based on an end-to-end\n1269\tmeasurement protocol that explicitly accounts for the auxiliary\n1270\tASR module’s overhead, including weight occupancy, initialization\n1271\tlatency, and real-time transcription costs.\n1272\tExperimental results demonstrate that VoxZip achieves optimal\n1273\tinference efficiency across the evaluated dimensions. As shown\n1274\tin Table 6, in the fixed 64K context evaluation, VoxZip exhibits\n1275\tsuperior memory efficiency, requiring a peak memory of 70.68 GB,\n1276\twhich yields a 3.34× memory reduction compared to the Full Cache\n1277\tbaseline. Furthermore, benefiting from the first-stage semantic anchored compression, the initial KV cache of semantic audio intervals\n1278\tis reduced to approximately 25% of its original capacity. This substantial compression significantly accelerates the prefill process,\n1279\t\n1280\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1281\t\n1282\tWenxu Jia et al.\n1283\t\n1284\tTable 4: Ablation of Stage-1 fusion strategies. Accuracy (%). Configurations: (1) T (Text-only): ASR transcriptions only; (2) A\n1285\t(Audio-only): raw audio only; (3) T+A (Partial): speech segments only, background discarded; (4) T+A (Full): our standard fusion.\n1286\tAcoustic Gain: absolute gain of T+A (Full) over T (Text-only).\n1287\tMMAR (Layer-wise)\n1288\t\n1289\tMMSU (Acoustic/Trait)\n1290\t\n1291\tSign.\n1292\t\n1293\tPerc.\n1294\t\n1295\tCult.\n1296\t\n1297\tPhon.\n1298\t\n1299\tStyle\n1300\t\n1301\tTrait\n1302\t\n1303\tSnd.\n1304\t\n1305\tMus.\n1306\t\n1307\t(1) T (Text-only)\n1308\t(2) A (Audio-only)\n1309\t\n1310\t46.51\n1311\t69.77\n1312\t\n1313\t55.45\n1314\t69.31\n1315\t\n1316\t58.39\n1317\t73.72\n1318\t\n1319\t60.27\n1320\t69.97\n1321\t\n1322\t47.28\n1323\t54.03\n1324\t\n1325\t30.46\n1326\t35.35\n1327\t\n1328\t65.47\n1329\t80.48\n1330\t\n1331\t(3) T+A (Partial)\n1332\t(4) T+A (Full)\n1333\t\n1334\t55.81\n1335\t61.54\n1336\t\n1337\t59.90\n1338\t67.24\n1339\t\n1340\t67.88\n1341\t72.99\n1342\t\n1343\t60.06\n1344\t63.72\n1345\t\n1346\t48.70\n1347\t56.74\n1348\t\n1349\t43.06\n1350\t37.53\n1351\t\n1352\tAcoustic Gain\n1353\t\n1354\t+15.03\n1355\t\n1356\t+11.79\n1357\t\n1358\t+9.47\n1359\t\n1360\t+3.50\n1361\t\n1362\t+9.53\n1363\t\n1364\t+7.07\n1365\t\n1366\tConfiguration\n1367\t\n1368\tTable 5: Sensitivity to the temporal decay factor (𝛾) on VoxInfinity under a 5% KV cache budget. Accuracy (%). 𝛾 = 1.0\n1369\tdenotes the baseline without temporal decay. Bold: best.\n1370\tDecay Factor (𝛾)\n1371\t\n1372\tPM\n1373\t\n1374\tUMT\n1375\t\n1376\tConv\n1377\t\n1378\tBS\n1379\t\n1380\tAvg.\n1381\t\n1382\t𝛾 = 0.80\n1383\t𝛾 = 0.90\n1384\t𝛾 = 0.95\n1385\t𝛾 = 0.99\n1386\t𝛾 = 1.00\n1387\t\n1388\t68.40\n1389\t73.20\n1390\t76.50\n1391\t67.10\n1392\t62.60\n1393\t\n1394\t35.10\n1395\t36.40\n1396\t37.00\n1397\t37.50\n1398\t37.80\n1399\t\n1400\t96.90\n1401\t97.20\n1402\t97.60\n1403\t97.00\n1404\t96.80\n1405\t\n1406\t20.30\n1407\t21.40\n1408\t22.00\n1409\t20.60\n1410\t19.80\n1411\t\n1412\t55.17\n1413\t57.05\n1414\t58.28\n1415\t55.55\n1416\t54.25\n1417\t\n1418\tTable 6: Efficiency at 64K context, 300 output tokens, 25%\n1419\tbudget, on 8×A100 (80GB). Mem (peak memory) and TPS\n1420\t(tokens per second) include ASR overhead; Prefill is the LLM\n1421\tprefill latency. Bold: best.\n1422\tPrefill (s) ↓\n1423\t\n1424\tMem (GB) ↓\n1425\t\n1426\tTPS (Speedup) ↑\n1427\t\n1428\tFull KV\n1429\t\n1430\t2.26\n1431\t\n1432\t235.93\n1433\t\n1434\t2.10 (1.00×)\n1435\t\n1436\tSnapKV\n1437\tPyramidKV\n1438\tChunkKV\n1439\t\n1440\t1.62\n1441\t1.66\n1442\t1.67\n1443\t\n1444\t80.04\n1445\t89.66\n1446\t79.98\n1447\t\n1448\t3.23 (1.54×)\n1449\t2.87 (1.37×)\n1450\t3.52 (1.68×)\n1451\t\n1452\tVoxZip (Ours)\n1453\t\n1454\t1.30\n1455\t\n1456\t70.68\n1457\t\n1458\t4.06 (1.93×)\n1459\t\n1460\tbringing the latency down to 1.30 seconds (a 1.74× speedup) and\n1461\tthereby outperforming mainstream methods such as SnapKV and\n1462\tPyramidKV.\n1463\tThe dynamic memory profile during multi-turn conversation\n1464\t(Figure 3) further illustrates the architectural advantages of VoxZip.\n1465\tAs conversational turns accumulate, the memory footprint of Full\n1466\tCache exhibits linear growth, eventually leading to potential outof-memory risks. Although SnapKV and PyramidKV stabilize in\n1467\tlater turns, their lack of token compression during the prefill stage\n1468\tresults in notable peak memory overhead due to the large volume of\n1469\tinitial input tokens. In contrast, VoxZip reduces the sequence length\n1470\tdirectly at the input source via preemptive semantic-anchored compression. Combined with the second-stage dynamic compression\n1471\t\n1472\tAM (Subset)\n1473\t\n1474\tVox\n1475\t\n1476\tSpch.\n1477\t\n1478\tSR\n1479\t\n1480\tAC\n1481\t\n1482\tBS\n1483\t\n1484\t61.38\n1485\t74.25\n1486\t\n1487\t75.38\n1488\t78.08\n1489\t\n1490\t61.34\n1491\t50.56\n1492\t\n1493\t52.25\n1494\t66.90\n1495\t\n1496\t21.80\n1497\t31.60\n1498\t\n1499\t53.00\n1500\t62.84\n1501\t\n1502\t78.39\n1503\t80.78\n1504\t\n1505\t73.05\n1506\t75.45\n1507\t\n1508\t71.17\n1509\t75.48\n1510\t\n1511\t53.47\n1512\t62.89\n1513\t\n1514\t53.67\n1515\t54.63\n1516\t\n1517\t23.80\n1518\t25.20\n1519\t\n1520\t57.41\n1521\t60.76\n1522\t\n1523\t+15.31\n1524\t\n1525\t+14.10\n1526\t\n1527\t+0.10\n1528\t\n1529\t+1.55\n1530\t\n1531\t+2.40\n1532\t\n1533\t+3.40\n1534\t\n1535\t+7.80\n1536\t\n1537\tAvg.\n1538\t\n1539\tmechanism that further condenses historical tokens, VoxZip maintains a gradual peak memory growth, ultimately plateauing at approximately 66 GB. This approach effectively lowers the hardware\n1540\tmemory requirements for sustained multi-turn conversation.\n1541\t\n1542\t5\n1543\t\n1544\tLimitations\n1545\t\n1546\tThe proposed VoxZip has two main limitations. Firstly, its reliance\n1547\ton ASR transcriptions as semantic anchors introduces vulnerability\n1548\tto noise and heavy accents; while our confidence-filtering strategy mitigates this, it cannot completely eliminate the impact of\n1549\tlow-quality transcriptions. Secondly, the element-wise addition\n1550\tused for audio-text fusion is inherently simplistic and may not adequately model complex cross-modal dynamics, suggesting that\n1551\tintegrating learnable fusion mechanisms could offer substantial\n1552\tfuture improvements.\n1553\t\n1554\t6\n1555\tMethod\n1556\t\n1557\tMMAU (Task)\n1558\t\n1559\tConclusion\n1560\t\n1561\tIn this paper, we investigate the modal heterogeneity phenomenon\n1562\twhere audio attention scores exhibit low information density relative to text. We propose VoxZip, a train-free audio KV cache compression framework that accelerates prefill and decoding. VoxZip\n1563\toperates in two stages: in the first stage, we use ASR transcriptions\n1564\tas semantic anchors to compress corresponding audio intervals and\n1565\tfuse them with text embeddings, increasing the information density\n1566\tof the audio semantic segments. In the second stage, we filter out\n1567\tlow-information tokens by employing temporally decayed accumulated attention scores as a contribution metric, which further\n1568\treduces low-density tokens and raises the overall compression ratio. Benchmark results on the Qwen3-Omni architecture across six\n1569\taudio tasks demonstrate that VoxZip consistently preserves holistic\n1570\tperception under high compression ratios. Notably, our method\n1571\tretains over 90% of the original performance under a 20× KV cache\n1572\tcompression ratio. At a 4× KV cache compression ratio, we achieve\n1573\tup to a 3.3× peak memory reduction and a 1.9× inference speedup.\n1574\t\n1575\tAcknowledgments\n1576\tThis work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant No. U25B2064, the “Pioneer” and “Leading Goose” R&D Program of Zhejiang under Grant No. 2025C02110,\n1577\tthe Public Welfare Research Program of Ningbo under Grant No.\n1578\t\n1579\t\fVoxZip: Semantic-Anchored Audio Compression\n1580\t\n1581\t2024S062, and the Yongjiang Talent Project of Ningbo under Grant\n1582\tNo. 2024A-161-G.\n1583\tThis research was also supported by Meituan.\n1584\t\n1585\tReferences\n1586\t[1] Orevaoghene Ahia, Martijn Bartelds, Kabir Ahuja, Hila Gonen, Valentin Hofmann,\n1587\tSiddhant Arora, Shuyue Stella Li, Vishal Puttagunta, Mofetoluwa Adeyemi, Charishma Buchireddy, Ben Walls, Noah Bennett, Shinji Watanabe, Noah A. Smith,\n1588\tYulia Tsvetkov, and Sachin Kumar. 2025. BLAB: Brutally Long Audio Bench.\n1589\tarXiv:2505.03054 [cs.AI] https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.03054\n1590\t[2] Zefan Cai, Yichi Zhang, Bofei Gao, Yuliang Liu, Yucheng Li, Tianyu Liu, Keming Lu, Wayne Xiong, Yue Dong, Junjie Hu, and Wen Xiao. 2025. PyramidKV:\n1591\tDynamic KV Cache Compression based on Pyramidal Information Funneling.\n1592\tarXiv:2406.02069 [cs.CL] https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.02069\n1593\t[3] Di Cao, Dongjie Fu, Hai Yu, Siqi Zheng, Xu Tan, and Tao Jin. 2026. X-OPD:\n1594\tCross-Modal On-Policy Distillation for Capability Alignment in Speech LLMs.\n1595\tarXiv:2603.24596 [eess.AS] https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24596\n1596\t[4] Liang Chen, Haozhe Zhao, Tianyu Liu, Shuai Bai, Junyang Lin, Chang Zhou,\n1597\tand Baobao Chang. 2024. An Image is Worth 1/2 Tokens After Layer\n1598\t2: Plug-and-Play Inference Acceleration for Large Vision-Language Models.\n1599\tarXiv:2403.06764 [cs.CV] https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06764\n1600\t[5] Xize Cheng, Dongjie Fu, Chenyuhao Wen, Tao Jin, Hai Yu, Di Cao, Qiny